  <section class="wrap prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">For teachers</p>
    <h1>Cocaine hippos in the classroom</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Pablo Escobar's escaped hippos are a story students remember. They are also a real case study in invasive
      species, ecosystem engineering and evolution. Everything on this site is free to use in teaching.
    </p>

    <h2>Topics it covers</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Invasive species:</strong> how a few animals became hundreds, and why removing them is hard.</li>
      <li><strong>Ecosystems:</strong> how hippos move nutrients from land to water and change oxygen levels in lakes.</li>
      <li><strong>Evolution:</strong> founder effects, isolation, island dwarfism, convergent evolution and the time scales involved.</li>
      <li><strong>Source criticism:</strong> every page separates established facts from plausible and speculative claims.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Three classroom activities</h2>
    <h3>1. Fact or speculation? (20 minutes)</h3>
    <p>
      Students read <a href="/hippos/">the facts page</a> and one <a href="/tree/">future species</a>. They mark
      which statements are backed by a source, which are reasoned guesses, and what evidence would change their mind.
    </p>
    <h3>2. Design a descendant (one lesson)</h3>
    <p>
      In groups, students pick an <a href="/hippos/1ma/">open niche</a> and design a species that fills it. They must
      name its ancestor, a real-world analogue and the selection pressure that shaped it. Good designs can be
      <a href="/contribute/">submitted</a> and credited to the class.
    </p>
    <h3>3. The management debate (one lesson)</h3>
    <p>
      Using the sources on <a href="/hippos/">the facts page</a>, students argue for sterilisation, relocation, culling
      or no action. Then they discuss how each choice changes the evolutionary future on this site.
    </p>

    <h2>Suitable for</h2>
    <p>Biology and environmental science, roughly ages 13–19. The texts are in English and can double as reading practice in English as a second language.</p>
    <p class="muted">Using this in a class? Your students' species are welcome as <a href="/contribute/?context=classroom">submissions</a>, credited to the class.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
